WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:08.420
THOMAS PATTERSON: Etched in stone in the rotunda
of the Capitol are the words "Here, sir, the

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.960
people govern."

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.769
Those words were spoken by Alexander Hamilton
when he asked what Congress represented.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:20.340
To the framers of the Constitution, the Congress
was the first branch of government, the place

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.619
where the people's representatives met to
decide the people's business.

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:30.210
Yet when the Constitution was unveiled, many
leading Americans were outraged.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.290
One of them was Richard Henry Lee of Virginia,
who had been president of the Continental

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.219
Congress during the Revolutionary War.

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:42.299
Richard Henry Lee said, "This Constitution
has very little democracy in it."

00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:46.090
What he was objecting to was the fact that
the people were not allowed to vote for the

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.969
president or for members of the Senate.

00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:52.929
Only the House of Representatives would be
chosen by a vote of the people.

00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:58.969
When Henry Clay of Kentucky, one of the great
legislators of the 19th century, left the

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:05.379
Senate to run for the House, he said, "I prefer
to serve in the people's chamber."

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:09.409
Some critics today wonder whether that label
still applies to the House.

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:14.060
They point to House elections, which have
become increasingly expensive.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.899
It was not that long ago that these were relatively
modest affairs, funded largely by donations

00:01:19.899 --> 00:01:22.390
from people living in the district.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.799
Today, it's easily the case that a House election
can cost $1 million or more, with much of

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:33.180
that money coming from wealthy individuals
and groups from outside the district.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:39.229
Critics also point to redistricting, the tendency
of state legislatures to pack House districts

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:43.909
with voters from a particular party, thereby
guaranteeing the election of that party's

00:01:43.909 --> 00:01:46.200
candidate in the fall.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.950
So who's doing the electing?

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.560
Is it the voters?

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.189
Or is it the state legislatures and the wealthy
donors?

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:57.979
Those kinds of issues about self-government
have been part of the American debate since

00:01:57.979 --> 00:01:59.341
the writing of the Constitution.

00:01:59.341 --> 00:02:00.341
[MUSIC PLAYING] Over the course of American
history, few features of the Constitution

00:02:00.341 --> 00:02:01.341
have been subject to more debate or more manipulation
than its provisions for representative government.

00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:02.341
In this session, we'll look at the Constitution's
provisions for representative government,

00:02:02.341 --> 00:02:03.341
a government in which the people rule through
their representatives.

00:02:03.341 --> 00:02:04.341
We'll start by examining the Constitution's
initial provisions for representation and

00:02:04.341 --> 00:02:05.341
then explain how and why they were changed.

00:02:05.341 --> 00:02:06.341
We'll then turn to voting laws and how they
have affected the public's role.

00:02:06.341 --> 00:02:07.341
Finally, we'll look at a couple of current
issues, voter ID laws and congressional redistricting.

00:02:07.341 --> 00:02:08.341
Before I start, I have a question for you
to think about.

00:02:08.341 --> 00:02:09.341
In your view, what's the bigger threat to
how democracy in America is practiced?

00:02:09.341 --> 00:02:10.341
Is it public officials, those who set the
rules for elections, or is it the people themselves,

00:02:10.341 --> 00:02:11.341
who can weaken democracy by not bothering
to vote or voting even when they're not eligible

00:02:11.341 --> 00:02:12.341
to vote?

00:02:12.341 --> 00:02:13.341
I'm not going to answer the question for you.

00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:14.341
I want you to think about it.

00:02:14.341 --> 00:02:15.341
When the delegates gathered in Philadelphia
in 1787 to write the Constitution, they had

00:02:15.341 --> 00:02:16.341
a number of factors that had to be taken into
account.

00:02:16.341 --> 00:02:17.341
Their first was that the states had their
own governments and intended to keep them.

00:02:17.341 --> 00:02:18.341
In addition, the states differed greatly in
size and wealth.

00:02:18.341 --> 00:02:19.341
Powerful states like Virginia and New York
were not interested in a union that failed

00:02:19.341 --> 00:02:20.341
to recognize their prominent role.

00:02:20.341 --> 00:02:21.341
At the same time, small states like Rhode
Island and Delaware were not interested in

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.341
a union where their interests would be pushed
aside.

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:23.341
There was also the question of the role the
people would play.

00:02:23.341 --> 00:02:24.341
Under the nation's first constitution, the
Articles of Confederation, members of Congress

00:02:24.341 --> 00:02:25.341
were chosen by the states.

00:02:25.341 --> 00:02:26.341
Voters had no say whatsoever in the selection
of their representatives.

00:02:26.341 --> 00:02:27.341
That would not work under the new constitution.

00:02:27.341 --> 00:02:28.341
The national government would have the power
to tax the people.

00:02:28.341 --> 00:02:29.341
They would likely rebel if denied all say
in the election of federal representatives.

00:02:29.341 --> 00:02:30.341
These considerations were resolved by what
has come to be called the Great Compromise,

00:02:30.341 --> 00:02:31.341
the decision to create a two-chambered Congress,
the House and the Senate.

00:02:31.341 --> 00:02:32.341
The House would have members in proportion
to each state's population, elected by the

00:02:32.341 --> 00:02:33.341
voters to two-year terms.

00:02:33.341 --> 00:02:34.341
This arrangement satisfied the larger states--
they would have a bigger share of House seats--

00:02:34.341 --> 00:02:35.341
and also served to satisfy those who believed
the people should have a direct vote.

00:02:35.341 --> 00:02:36.341
In contrast, the Senate would have two members
per state, chosen by the state legislatures

00:02:36.341 --> 00:02:37.341
for six-year terms.

00:02:37.341 --> 00:02:38.341
This arrangement satisfied the concerns of
small states.

00:02:38.341 --> 00:02:39.341
They would have equal representation in the
Senate.

00:02:39.341 --> 00:02:40.341
It also mollified those who wanted the states
to have a say in the selection of federal

00:02:40.341 --> 00:02:41.341
officials.

00:02:41.341 --> 00:02:42.341
State interests also figured into the method
of choosing the president.

00:02:42.341 --> 00:02:43.341
Pennsylvania's James Wilson twice proposed
that the president be popularly elected.

00:02:43.341 --> 00:02:44.341
Twice the delegates voted him down.

00:02:44.341 --> 00:02:45.341
A proposal to have Congress choose the president
was also rejected.

00:02:45.341 --> 00:02:46.341
That method would have weakened the president's
ability to act as a check on the Congress.

00:02:46.341 --> 00:02:47.341
Congress would pick presidents who promised
to do its bidding.

00:02:47.341 --> 00:02:48.341
Having pretty much run out of ideas, the framers
invented what has come to be called the Electoral

00:02:48.341 --> 00:02:49.341
College.

00:02:49.341 --> 00:02:50.341
Each state would have as many electors as
it had members of Congress, House and Senate

00:02:50.341 --> 00:02:51.341
combined.

00:02:51.341 --> 00:02:52.341
The state legislatures would choose the electors,
and the candidate with the majority of the

00:02:52.341 --> 00:02:53.341
electoral votes would become president.

00:02:53.341 --> 00:02:54.341
Thomas Jefferson was 3,000 miles away when
the Constitution was written, serving as US

00:02:54.341 --> 00:02:55.341
Ambassador to France.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.341
He admired many of the Constitution's features,
particularly its separation of powers.

00:02:56.341 --> 00:02:57.341
But Jefferson worried that the people had
been given too little power.

00:02:57.341 --> 00:02:58.341
Jefferson also thought officials should be
term limited.

00:02:58.341 --> 00:02:59.341
If not, they might come to believe that public
office was theirs by right.

00:02:59.341 --> 00:03:00.341
Other critics noted the contrast between the
Constitution's provisions and what the states

00:03:00.341 --> 00:03:01.341
were doing.

00:03:01.341 --> 00:03:02.341
In nearly all states, governors and legislators
were chosen by direct popular vote.

00:03:02.341 --> 00:03:03.341
Why should the national government be any
different?

00:03:03.341 --> 00:03:04.341
In defending the Constitution's provisions,
the framers argued that the history of unrestrained

00:03:04.341 --> 00:03:05.341
democracies was the history of tyranny, where
the many trampled on the rights of the few.

00:03:05.341 --> 00:03:06.341
Said James Madison, "Pure democracies have
ever been spectacles of turbulence and contention

00:03:06.341 --> 00:03:07.341
and have in general been as short in their
lives as they have been violent in their deaths."

00:03:07.341 --> 00:03:08.341
The framers even had a homegrown example,
Rhode Island, to prove their point.

00:03:08.341 --> 00:03:09.341
In that state, debtors had gained control
of the legislature and concocted a scheme

00:03:09.341 --> 00:03:10.341
for getting rid of their debt.

00:03:10.341 --> 00:03:11.341
They passed a law authorizing the use of paper
money, which was largely worthless, to pay

00:03:11.341 --> 00:03:12.341
debts incurred by borrowing gold.

00:03:12.341 --> 00:03:13.341
Creditors were hunted down, tied up in public
places, and people lined up to pay off their

00:03:13.341 --> 00:03:14.341
debts with the worthless paper money.

00:03:14.341 --> 00:03:15.341
Soon the Rhode Island economy was a wreck,
with inflation running at 800%.

00:03:15.341 --> 00:03:16.341
Most of its shops had shut down, and creditors
had fled the state.

00:03:16.341 --> 00:03:17.341
A Boston newspaper labeled the state Rogue
Island.

00:03:17.341 --> 00:03:18.341
The framers were so convinced of the dangers
of democracy that they refused even to call

00:03:18.341 --> 00:03:19.341
the new government a democracy.

00:03:19.341 --> 00:03:20.341
They called it instead a republic.

00:03:20.341 --> 00:03:21.341
The people would have a voice in the government,
but it would be filtered through representative

00:03:21.341 --> 00:03:22.341
institutions, only one of which, the House,
would be subject to direct election.

00:03:22.341 --> 00:03:23.341
The Constitution, said Madison, was "a republican
remedy for the ills of uncontrolled democracy."

00:03:23.341 --> 00:03:24.341
Now whether intended or not, the Constitution's
provisions for representative government virtually

00:03:24.341 --> 00:03:25.341
guaranteed that the federal government would
initially be controlled by wealthy or otherwise

00:03:25.341 --> 00:03:26.341
prominent Americans.

00:03:26.341 --> 00:03:27.341
Through the first six presidents, very few
small farmers, craftsmen, or merchants were

00:03:27.341 --> 00:03:28.341
elected to Congress or held high-ranking executive
positions, even though they made up the vast

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:29.341
majority of the population.

00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:30.341
That situation changed with the election of
the seventh president, Andrew Jackson.

00:03:30.341 --> 00:03:31.341
Raised in poverty by a widowed mother in the
backwoods of Carolina, Jackson won national

00:03:31.341 --> 00:03:32.341
fame as commanding general at the Battle of
New Orleans, where US forces defeated the

00:03:32.341 --> 00:03:33.341
British in the War of 1812.

00:03:33.341 --> 00:03:34.341
Upon assuming the presidency, Jackson appointed
ordinary citizens to executive office and

00:03:34.341 --> 00:03:35.341
pressured states to eliminate property ownership
as a requirement for voting.

00:03:35.341 --> 00:03:36.341
He tried but failed to get rid of the Electoral
College but then persuaded the states to tie

00:03:36.341 --> 00:03:37.341
their electoral votes to the popular vote.

00:03:37.341 --> 00:03:38.341
Jackson's system is the one we have today.

00:03:38.341 --> 00:03:39.341
The winner of a state's popular vote receives
its electoral votes, giving the voters a decisive

00:03:39.341 --> 00:03:40.341
say in the choice of the president.

00:03:40.341 --> 00:03:41.341
Seven decades later, the Constitution was
amended to provide for the popular election

00:03:41.341 --> 00:03:42.341
of senators.

00:03:42.341 --> 00:03:43.341
The Senate had become a corrupt institution.

00:03:43.341 --> 00:03:44.341
Powerful corporations had bribed and bullied
state legislatures to choose senators who

00:03:44.341 --> 00:03:45.341
would protect their interests.

00:03:45.341 --> 00:03:46.341
The ensuing scandals helped fuel the Progressive
movement, which was the leading force behind

00:03:46.341 --> 00:03:47.341
the amendment.

00:03:47.341 --> 00:03:48.341
The Progressives also challenged the party
machines that had taken over control of candidate

00:03:48.341 --> 00:03:49.341
nominations.

00:03:49.341 --> 00:03:50.341
They succeeded in getting states to adopt
primary elections, where the voters rather

00:03:50.341 --> 00:03:51.341
than the party organizations chose the nominees.

00:03:51.341 --> 00:03:52.341
As you might have noted, these changes basically
undid the work of the framers.

00:03:52.341 --> 00:03:53.341
The presidency, House, and Senate were now
all subject to vote of the people, who had

00:03:53.341 --> 00:03:54.341
two chances to be heard, once in the primary
and once in the general election.

00:03:54.341 --> 00:03:55.341
This was a remarkable change.

00:03:55.341 --> 00:03:56.341
At the beginning of the republic, the people
had a relatively small voice in their governing.

00:03:56.341 --> 00:03:57.341
Today, as has been true ever since the Progressive
Era, the United States has more elections

00:03:57.341 --> 00:03:58.341
for national office than any other democracy.

00:03:58.341 --> 00:03:59.341
Lots of elections doesn't mean that Americans
vote regularly.

00:03:59.341 --> 00:04:00.341
In fact, the United States has nearly the
lowest voting rate among Western democracies.

00:04:00.341 --> 00:04:01.341
As this chart indicates, the voting rate in
US presidential elections is roughly 60%,

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:02.341
below that of other leading democracies.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:03.341
In some countries, Italy being an example,
the rate exceeds 80%.

00:04:03.341 --> 00:04:04.341
To understand why the United States lags behind,
we need to go back to the writing of the Constitution.

00:04:04.341 --> 00:04:05.341
As originally written, the Constitution did
not provide for the right to vote.

00:04:05.341 --> 00:04:06.341
Some Americans were held in slavery and were
denied even citizenship.

00:04:06.341 --> 00:04:07.341
For their part, women were thought not to
have a rightful place in public affairs.

00:04:07.341 --> 00:04:08.341
Not until ratification of the 19th Amendment
in 1920 did women get the right to vote.

00:04:08.341 --> 00:04:09.341
The original Constitution had a single clause
on voting.

00:04:09.341 --> 00:04:10.341
It gave the states authority to determine
voting eligibility in federal elections, saying

00:04:10.341 --> 00:04:11.341
only that a state's restrictions on voting
could not be greater than those placed on

00:04:11.341 --> 00:04:12.341
voting for members of the largest chamber
of the state's legislature.

00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:13.341
That clause seemed harmless at the time.

00:04:13.341 --> 00:04:14.341
But it has turned out to be a source of mischief.

00:04:14.341 --> 00:04:15.341
It has been used at times to deny some people
the right to vote.

00:04:15.341 --> 00:04:16.341
In the early history of the United States,
most states denied the vote to males without

00:04:16.341 --> 00:04:17.341
property, a policy that Ben Franklin thought
worthy of ridicule.

00:04:17.341 --> 00:04:18.341
"Today," wrote Franklin, "a man owns a jackass
worth $50 and he is entitled to vote.

00:04:18.341 --> 00:04:19.341
But before the next election, the jackass
dies and the man cannot vote.

00:04:19.341 --> 00:04:20.341
Who's the voter, the man or the jackass?"

00:04:20.341 --> 00:04:21.341
By the 1830s, urged on by Andrew Jackson,
nearly all states had dropped the property

00:04:21.341 --> 00:04:22.341
requirement.

00:04:22.341 --> 00:04:23.341
The ugliest chapter in America's voting history
began in the South after the Civil War and

00:04:23.341 --> 00:04:24.341
lasted nearly a century.

00:04:24.341 --> 00:04:25.341
The 15th Amendment, which was ratified soon
after the war, prohibits states from denying

00:04:25.341 --> 00:04:26.341
the vote on the basis of race or color.

00:04:26.341 --> 00:04:27.341
When Northern troops left the South a decade
after the Civil War ended, the South's white-majority

00:04:27.341 --> 00:04:28.341
governments concocted every trick imaginable
to keep blacks from voting.

00:04:28.341 --> 00:04:29.341
Louisiana wiped clean its registration rolls
and required its residents to re-register.

00:04:29.341 --> 00:04:30.341
The state made it so hard for blacks to re-register
that less than one in 10 were able to do so.

00:04:30.341 --> 00:04:31.341
Throughout the South, literacy tests and poll
taxes were adopted.

00:04:31.341 --> 00:04:32.341
In order to register to vote, citizens had
to pass a literacy test and pay a tax.

00:04:32.341 --> 00:04:33.341
To ensure that white voters were not disenfranchised
by these requirements, a grandfather clause

00:04:33.341 --> 00:04:34.341
was placed in the law.

00:04:34.341 --> 00:04:35.341
Anyone whose father or grandfather had been
eligible to vote was exempt from the requirements.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:36.341
Obviously, no former slave or child of a slave
qualified for that exemption.

00:04:36.341 --> 00:04:37.341
The Supreme Court waited until 1915 before
declaring the scheme unconstitutional.

00:04:37.341 --> 00:04:38.341
Whites-only primaries were also devised.

00:04:38.341 --> 00:04:39.341
The Democratic Party was in control throughout
the South.

00:04:39.341 --> 00:04:40.341
Every governor, every state legislature, and
nearly every member of Congress was a Democrat.

00:04:40.341 --> 00:04:41.341
As a result, the key election throughout the
South was the Democratic primary.

00:04:41.341 --> 00:04:42.341
Whoever won that primary was virtually assured
of winning the general election.

00:04:42.341 --> 00:04:43.341
Blacks were prohibited from voting in primaries.

00:04:43.341 --> 00:04:44.341
The Supreme Court waited until 1944 to strike
down that practice.

00:04:44.341 --> 00:04:45.341
The Court refused, however, to strike down
literacy tests and poll taxes, and they effectively

00:04:45.341 --> 00:04:46.341
kept blacks away from the polls.

00:04:46.341 --> 00:04:47.341
The literacy tests were so difficult that
those administering them had to look up the

00:04:47.341 --> 00:04:48.341
answers and were given discretion in determining
whether an applicant had passed.

00:04:48.341 --> 00:04:49.341
Whites routinely did.

00:04:49.341 --> 00:04:50.341
Blacks who scored as high or higher were frequently
told they had failed.

00:04:50.341 --> 00:04:51.341
In some places, it took courage even to apply
for the vote.

00:04:51.341 --> 00:04:52.341
The local newspaper would publish the names
of those who had applied so that white employers

00:04:52.341 --> 00:04:53.341
would know which blacks not to hire.

00:04:53.341 --> 00:04:54.341
In the entire state of Mississippi, fewer
than 5% of eligible blacks were registered

00:04:54.341 --> 00:04:55.341
to vote even as late as the 1950s.

00:04:55.341 --> 00:04:56.341
The Voting Rights Act of 1965 brought an end
to these tricks.

00:04:56.341 --> 00:04:57.341
The legislation empowered federal officials
to register voters in states and counties

00:04:57.341 --> 00:04:58.341
with histories of racial discrimination.

00:04:58.341 --> 00:04:59.341
In less than two years, black registration
in Mississippi had jumped tenfold.

00:04:59.341 --> 00:05:00.341
In Alabama, it climbed from less than 20%
to over 50%.

00:05:00.341 --> 00:05:01.341
The Voting Rights Act produced a regional
split in Congress not seen since.

00:05:01.341 --> 00:05:02.341
The Senate vote was 77 to 19, with all 19
no votes cast by Southern senators.

00:05:02.341 --> 00:05:03.341
Every senator from outside the South, Republican
or Democrat, voted for the bill.

00:05:03.341 --> 00:05:04.341
The House vote also divided sharply along
North-South lines.

00:05:04.341 --> 00:05:05.341
Once the law was in place, southern states
challenged it in court, arguing it violated

00:05:05.341 --> 00:05:06.341
the constitutional clause that gives states
the power to determine voter eligibility.

00:05:06.341 --> 00:05:07.341
The Supreme Court rejected the challenge,
holding that another clause of the Constitution,

00:05:07.341 --> 00:05:08.341
the 14th Amendment's equal protection clause,
took precedent.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:09.341
The Voting Rights Act also temporarily banned
the use of literacy tests, which were later

00:05:09.341 --> 00:05:10.341
banned completely by the Supreme Court.

00:05:10.341 --> 00:05:11.341
Ratification of the 24th Amendment brought
the poll tax to an end.

00:05:11.341 --> 00:05:12.341
Since passage of the Voting Rights Act in
1965, black voting has edged steadily upward

00:05:12.341 --> 00:05:13.341
such that today the rate is not greatly different
from that of white voters.

00:05:13.341 --> 00:05:14.341
Now if the politics of voting until the 1960s
were largely the politics of race, it has

00:05:14.341 --> 00:05:15.341
since been a case of party politics as the
Republican and Democratic parties have jockeyed

00:05:15.341 --> 00:05:16.341
for voting regulations that might help them
win elections.

00:05:16.341 --> 00:05:17.341
A major target has been voter registration,
which is handled differently in the United

00:05:17.341 --> 00:05:18.341
States than in Europe.

00:05:18.341 --> 00:05:19.341
In European democracies, government assumes
responsibility for registering voters.

00:05:19.341 --> 00:05:20.341
If a citizen moves to a new address, for example,
the postal service will notify election officials

00:05:20.341 --> 00:05:21.341
to change the individual's registration.

00:05:21.341 --> 00:05:22.341
The result is that over 90% of eligible European
voters are registered.

00:05:22.341 --> 00:05:23.341
By comparison, only about 75% of vote-eligible
US citizens are registered.

00:05:23.341 --> 00:05:24.341
The difference stems from the fact that registration
in the United States is the individual's responsibility.

00:05:24.341 --> 00:05:25.341
Eligible citizens must personally take the
time to register.

00:05:25.341 --> 00:05:26.341
Where government comes into this picture is
that it can make that process easier or harder.

00:05:26.341 --> 00:05:27.341
If it lowers the barriers to registration,
you expand the electorate.

00:05:27.341 --> 00:05:28.341
If it raises them, you shrink it.

00:05:28.341 --> 00:05:29.341
So which political party do you think would
benefit from making registration easier?

00:05:29.341 --> 00:05:30.341
The Democrats, the Republicans, both about
equally?

00:05:30.341 --> 00:05:31.341
It's the Democrats.

00:05:31.341 --> 00:05:32.341
Voting in the United States tilts heavily
toward those of higher income.

00:05:32.341 --> 00:05:33.341
As you can see from this chart, the voting
rate among those in the top fifth of income

00:05:33.341 --> 00:05:34.341
is far higher than those in the bottom fifth.

00:05:34.341 --> 00:05:35.341
Income correlates with party preference.

00:05:35.341 --> 00:05:36.341
Upper income voters tend to cast their votes
for Republican candidates, while those at

00:05:36.341 --> 00:05:37.341
the bottom of the income ladder tend to vote
Democratic as a result of the party's leadership

00:05:37.341 --> 00:05:38.341
and policies that benefit those of lower income
such as welfare services and the minimum wage.

00:05:38.341 --> 00:05:39.341
The arithmetic of voting has not been lost
on the parties.

00:05:39.341 --> 00:05:40.341
The Democratic Party has pressed hard to make
voter registration easier.

00:05:40.341 --> 00:05:41.341
Its most notable success was the 1993 Motor
Voter Act.

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:42.341
The Act requires states to offer voter registrations
when they apply for a driver's license or

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:43.341
public assistance.

00:05:43.341 --> 00:05:44.341
The Motor Voter bill first passed Congress
in 1991 when a Republican, George HW Bush,

00:05:44.341 --> 00:05:45.341
was in the White House.

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:46.341
He vetoed it.

00:05:46.341 --> 00:05:47.341
The Democratic-controlled Congress passed
it again in 1993.

00:05:47.341 --> 00:05:48.341
This time a fellow Democrat, Bill Clinton,
was in office, and he signed the bill into

00:05:48.341 --> 00:05:49.341
law.

00:05:49.341 --> 00:05:50.341
In the Senate, only three Republicans backed
the bill, while all 59 Democrats did so.

00:05:50.341 --> 00:05:51.341
Voting in the House also divided sharply along
party lines, with Republicans overwhelmingly

00:05:51.341 --> 00:05:52.341
opposed and Democrats overwhelmingly in favor.

00:05:52.341 --> 00:05:53.341
Studies of the Motor Voter Act indicate it
accounts for about 1/3 of new registrants.

00:05:53.341 --> 00:05:54.341
To the surprise of both parties, however,
the number of new Democratic registrants resulting

00:05:54.341 --> 00:05:55.341
from the law has only slightly outpaced the
number of new Republican registrants.

00:05:55.341 --> 00:05:56.341
Now the Republican Party has worked the other
side of the fence, seeking to hold down voter

00:05:56.341 --> 00:05:57.341
registration.

00:05:57.341 --> 00:05:58.341
The centerpiece of this effort in recent years
has been voter ID laws.

00:05:58.341 --> 00:05:59.341
In strictest form, an applicant must have
a government-issued photo ID such as a driver's

00:05:59.341 --> 00:06:00.341
license or passport in order to register.

00:06:00.341 --> 00:06:01.341
In other states with voter ID laws, other
forms of identification are acceptable as

00:06:01.341 --> 00:06:02.341
long as they suggest the applicant is a citizen.

00:06:02.341 --> 00:06:03.341
Now the express purpose of these laws is to
prevent voter fraud, to keep people who are

00:06:03.341 --> 00:06:04.341
noncitizens from voting.

00:06:04.341 --> 00:06:05.341
The real intent is different.

00:06:05.341 --> 00:06:06.341
They are designed to make it difficult for
those without a formal ID to register.

00:06:06.341 --> 00:06:07.341
About 10% of vote-eligible citizens do not
have a government-issued identification card.

00:06:07.341 --> 00:06:08.341
And they are disproportionately young, poor,
and minorities.

00:06:08.341 --> 00:06:09.341
All of these groups tend to vote Democratic.

00:06:09.341 --> 00:06:10.341
The first voter ID laws were enacted by the
Republican-controlled legislatures of Georgia

00:06:10.341 --> 00:06:11.341
and Indiana.

00:06:11.341 --> 00:06:12.341
Of the roughly 30 states that have followed
their example, all but one, Rhode Island,

00:06:12.341 --> 00:06:13.341
had a Republican-controlled legislature.

00:06:13.341 --> 00:06:14.341
In a 6 to 3 ruling in 2008, the Supreme Court
upheld Indiana's law.

00:06:14.341 --> 00:06:15.341
All six judges in the majority were Republican
appointees.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:16.341
And though they acknowledged Indiana's law
had a partisan purpose, they said other purposes,

00:06:16.341 --> 00:06:17.341
including the public's faith in the integrity
of the ballot, justified the ID law.

00:06:17.341 --> 00:06:18.341
The Court said that the law "should not be
disregarded simply because partisan interests

00:06:18.341 --> 00:06:19.341
may have provided one motivation for the votes
of individual legislators."

00:06:19.341 --> 00:06:20.341
Research indicates that voter fraud is extremely
rare.

00:06:20.341 --> 00:06:21.341
It appears that almost no one is willing to
risk being arrested and fined for illegal

00:06:21.341 --> 00:06:22.341
voting.

00:06:22.341 --> 00:06:23.341
Sometimes it's even tough to get legal voters
to show up at the polls.

00:06:23.341 --> 00:06:24.341
On the other hand, research also indicates
that the suppressive effect of voter ID laws

00:06:24.341 --> 00:06:25.341
is smaller than many opponents of the laws
have claimed.

00:06:25.341 --> 00:06:26.341
It's still early in the history of these laws,
so their true impact is unlikely to be known

00:06:26.341 --> 00:06:27.341
for some time.

00:06:27.341 --> 00:06:28.341
Moreover, new legal challenges to voter ID
laws are currently working their way through

00:06:28.341 --> 00:06:29.341
the courts, which could mean that the Supreme
Court might at some point take a second look

00:06:29.341 --> 00:06:30.341
at the issue.

00:06:30.341 --> 00:06:31.341
Finally, let's briefly examine partisan gerrymandering,
a tactic used by both parties.

00:06:31.341 --> 00:06:32.341
After the population census every 10th year,
House seats are reapportioned to states on

00:06:32.341 --> 00:06:33.341
the basis of their populations.

00:06:33.341 --> 00:06:34.341
The more populous the state, the more House
seats it gets.

00:06:34.341 --> 00:06:35.341
California, for example, has 53 House seats
while South Dakota has only one.

00:06:35.341 --> 00:06:36.341
Under the Constitution, state legislatures
have the authority to set the boundaries of

00:06:36.341 --> 00:06:37.341
House districts.

00:06:37.341 --> 00:06:38.341
The majority party in these legislatures typically
divides up the state in a way that will help

00:06:38.341 --> 00:06:39.341
its party's candidates, a process called gerrymandering.

00:06:39.341 --> 00:06:40.341
The safe way to do this is to pack targeted
districts with enough of the party's voters

00:06:40.341 --> 00:06:41.341
to assure victory in the election.

00:06:41.341 --> 00:06:42.341
The other party is also likely to receive
safe seats.

00:06:42.341 --> 00:06:43.341
This happens because if some districts are
stacked to the top with the opposing party's

00:06:43.341 --> 00:06:44.341
votes, there will be fewer such voters in
other districts.

00:06:44.341 --> 00:06:45.341
More than 85% of House seats today are uncompetitive,
virtually certain to be won by the candidate

00:06:45.341 --> 00:06:46.341
of the stronger party.

00:06:46.341 --> 00:06:47.341
There are several reasons for this, including
the fact that some states are so heavily Democratic

00:06:47.341 --> 00:06:48.341
or Republican that it's hard to design competitive
districts.

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:49.341
But gerrymandering is among the reasons that
many House races are effectively decided before

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:50.341
a single vote is cast.

00:06:50.341 --> 00:06:51.341
Now gerrymandering worked to Democrats' advantage
until the early 1990s, at which time an increasing

00:06:51.341 --> 00:06:52.341
number of state legislatures came under Republican
control.

00:06:52.341 --> 00:06:53.341
Republican legislatures did such a precise
job of gerrymandering after the 2010 census

00:06:53.341 --> 00:06:54.341
that in the 2012 election, they won a comfortable
majority of House seats, even though Republican

00:06:54.341 --> 00:06:55.341
candidates received fewer votes nationally
than did their Democratic opponents.

00:06:55.341 --> 00:06:56.341
As gerrymandering has become ever more precise
as a result of computers, it has increasingly

00:06:56.341 --> 00:06:57.341
come under legal challenges.

00:06:57.341 --> 00:06:58.341
In 2004, the Supreme Court ruled on a case
centering on the redistricting plan enacted

00:06:58.341 --> 00:06:59.341
by Pennsylvania's Republican governor and
legislature.

00:06:59.341 --> 00:07:00.341
The suit alleged that the plan violated the
principle of one person, one vote and also

00:07:00.341 --> 00:07:01.341
violated the Constitution's equal protection
clause.

00:07:01.341 --> 00:07:02.341
The Republican-dominated Court ruled against
the challenge in a 5 to 4 vote, concluding

00:07:02.341 --> 00:07:03.341
that the judiciary is not equipped to draw
district lines.

00:07:03.341 --> 00:07:04.341
Any such attempt, said the Court's majority,
would require judges to make arbitrary decisions

00:07:04.341 --> 00:07:05.341
about district boundaries.

00:07:05.341 --> 00:07:06.341
Nevertheless, Justice Anthony Kennedy, who
voted with the majority, wrote that he would

00:07:06.341 --> 00:07:07.341
not foreclose the possibility of judicial
intervention in the future, saying that a

00:07:07.341 --> 00:07:08.341
truly outrageous districting plan, one that
carved a state into a set of bizarre-shaped

00:07:08.341 --> 00:07:09.341
districts, could trigger action by the Court.

00:07:09.341 --> 00:07:10.341
Now a few states, Iowa, California, and Montana
among them, have recently decided to place

00:07:10.341 --> 00:07:11.341
redistricting in the hands of a nonpartisan
commission.

00:07:11.341 --> 00:07:12.341
It's unlikely that a great many states will
follow their example, given the fact that

00:07:12.341 --> 00:07:13.341
most state legislatures have signaled their
intent to retain control of redistricting.

00:07:13.341 --> 00:07:14.341
Nevertheless, the Supreme Court in 2015 gave
voters in states that allow ballot initiatives

00:07:14.341 --> 00:07:15.341
a remedy if they choose to exercise it.

00:07:15.341 --> 00:07:16.341
The case centered on a redistricting commission
that Arizona's voters had created through

00:07:16.341 --> 00:07:17.341
a ballot initiative.

00:07:17.341 --> 00:07:18.341
The Arizona legislature brought suit, claiming
it alone had the constitutional authority

00:07:18.341 --> 00:07:19.341
to determine the boundaries of the state's
House districts.

00:07:19.341 --> 00:07:20.341
The Supreme Court ruled against the state
legislature and in favor of the state's voters.

00:07:20.341 --> 00:07:21.341
It said that Arizona's voters had been acting
in a legislative capacity when they created

00:07:21.341 --> 00:07:22.341
the redistricting commission.

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:23.341
OK, let's wrap up what we've covered in this
session.

00:07:23.341 --> 00:07:24.341
We focused on representative government as
provided by the Constitution.

00:07:24.341 --> 00:07:25.341
It was noted that the voters originally had
a say only in the election of House members,

00:07:25.341 --> 00:07:26.341
a reflection of the framers' determination
to prevent a passionate majority from taking

00:07:26.341 --> 00:07:27.341
control and running roughshod over the minority.

00:07:27.341 --> 00:07:28.341
With time, due in part to the Jacksonian and
Progressive movements, popular voting came

00:07:28.341 --> 00:07:29.341
to include Senate and presidential elections.

00:07:29.341 --> 00:07:30.341
The adoption of primary elections and American
invention also served to shift power to the

00:07:30.341 --> 00:07:31.341
voters.

00:07:31.341 --> 00:07:32.341
Today, Americans vote more often for more
offices than voters elsewhere.

00:07:32.341 --> 00:07:33.341
On the other hand, the public's influence
is diminished by partisan gerrymandering and

00:07:33.341 --> 00:07:34.341
by the efforts of officials to manipulate
the size of the electorate through control

00:07:34.341 --> 00:07:35.341
of the voter registration process.

00:07:35.341 --> 00:07:36.341
Although these recent efforts pale alongside
those used in the past to keep black Americans

00:07:36.341 --> 00:07:37.341
from voting, they are a source of court challenges
and intense partisan debate.

00:07:37.341 --> 00:07:37.354
In other words, representative government
in America is still a work in progress.

